<h1><span lang="EN-US">Type In Transformation</span></h1>
<hr align="center" size="5" class="whs3">
<p><span lang="EN-US">This command is accessible from</span> <b><i style="font-style:normal;"><span class="Path" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Transformations &gt; Create &gt; Type-In</b></span></i></b><span lang="EN-US">.</span></p>
<p class="description"><b><i style="font-weight:normal; font-style:normal;"><span class="Command" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Description:</b></span> <span class="Command">Type-In Transformation</span></i></b> <span lang="EN-US">command is used to directly set rotation/translation values or the transformation matrix.</span> <span lang="EN-GB">A transformation matrix defines how to map points from one co-ordinate space into another co-ordinate space. The default name of the entity is Transformation N.</span></p>
<p><img src="../images/image005.gif" width="366px" height="446px" border="0" class="img_whs4" alt=""></p>
<p class="parameterstitle"><span style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Rotate/Translate</b></span></p>
<p class="parameterdescription">Check this option to define the transformation by setting rotation and/or translation parameters.</p>
<p class="parameterstitle"><span style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Transformation matrix</b></span></p>
<p class="parameterdescription">Check this option to define the transformation by setting its matrix.</p>
<p class="whs5"><span lang="EN-GB">In the fields for the X, Y, Z normal you can input values in the range: -1.5 to 1.5. For the Translation fields there are no limits for the values.</span></p>
<p class="whs5"><span lang="EN-GB">B</span>y manually constructing a transformation matrix you can achieve various transformations like translation, rotation, scale, etc. The following are examples of different transformations:</p>
<p class="parameterstitle"><span style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Translation</b></span>:</p>
<p class="whs5"><img src="../images/Image006.gif" width="111px" height="100px" border="0" class="img_whs6" alt=""></p>
<p class="whs5">where Dx, Dy, and Dz are the offsets along the X, Y, and Z axes respectively.</p>
<p class="parameterstitle"><span style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Rotation</b></span>:</p>
<p class="whs5">&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;around X-axis &#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;around Y-axis &#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;&#160;around Z-axis</p>
<p class="whs5">&#160;<img src="../images/image008.gif" width="413px" height="111px" border="0" class="img_whs7" alt=""></p>
<p class="whs5">where <span style="font-family:Symbol, serif;">j, q, y</span> are the angles of rotation around the X, Y, and Z axes respectively.</p>
<p class="Note" id="P1"><span lang="EN-GB" style="font-weight:bold;"><b>Note:</b></span> <span lang="EN-GB">The matrix is automatically made orthogonal if it is not. This means that only rotation and translation modifications are allowed</span>.</p>
<p class="Use"><b style="font-weight:bold;">Use</b> : Create Transformation By Type-In when you know in advance the exact amount of rotation/translation or matrix of transformation.</p>
